𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟱𝟰/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗛𝗣𝗔 𝗶𝘀 𝗡𝗼𝘁 𝗦𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗣𝗼𝗱𝘀 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

Your application is experiencing high traffic, but the Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) is not increasing the number of replicas. How would you troubleshoot this in production?

Here’s my practical approach 👇

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗛𝗣𝗔 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝘂𝘀

kubectl get hpa -n <namespace>
kubectl describe hpa <hpa-name> -n <namespace>

Check:

- Current CPU/memory utilization
- Target utilization
- Minimum and maximum replicas
- Events and scaling conditions

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝗠𝗲𝘁𝗿𝗶𝗰𝘀 𝗔𝗣𝗜

kubectl top pods -n <namespace>
kubectl get apiservice v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io

If metrics are unavailable, investigate whether Metrics Server is installed and healthy.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗖𝗣𝗨 𝗥𝗲𝗾𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝘀

For CPU utilization-based HPA, CPU requests are important because utilization is calculated relative to the requested CPU.

kubectl describe deployment <deployment-name> -n <namespace>

Verify that CPU requests are configured correctly in the container specification.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗠𝗶𝗻 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗠𝗮𝘅 𝗥𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘀

kubectl get hpa <hpa-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml

Check:

- "minReplicas"
- "maxReplicas"
- "metrics"
- "scaleTargetRef"

Your HPA may already be at its maximum replica limit.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗖𝗹𝘂𝘀𝘁𝗲𝗿 𝗖𝗮𝗽𝗮𝗰𝗶𝘁𝘆

kubectl get deployment -n <namespace>
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl get nodes

Even if HPA requests more replicas, Pods may remain Pending if the cluster has insufficient resources.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗦𝗰𝗮𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗕𝗲𝗵𝗮𝘃𝗶𝗼𝘂𝗿

Review HPA scaling policies, stabilization windows, and scaling events. These settings can delay or limit scaling.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

HPA depends on metrics, resource requests, scaling configuration, and available cluster capacity.

More traffic does not automatically mean more Pods. You must verify the complete scaling path.
